<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the side-car speech engine, when it is signed with a real
  Developer ID.

  Deliberately a shorter list than tools/ClaudeBuddy.entitlements. The engine is
  a headless process that loads a model, synthesises audio and hands the samples
  to afplay; it sends no Apple Events and opens no input device, so the two
  TCC-gated keys the app needs would be privileges granted for nothing.

  What it does share is the reason the app needs entitlements at all: this is a
  self-contained .NET program, and signing it with the hardened runtime turns
  off things CoreCLR depends on. These are load-bearing — sign the engine with
  the hardened runtime and none of these, and the result installs correctly and
  then dies the moment the app tries to speak.

  (No double hyphens anywhere in these comments: codesign parses this through
  AMFIUnserializeXML, which rejects the XML that a "hyphen hyphen options"
  spelling of a codesign flag would produce, with only a line number to go on.)
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- CoreCLR JITs managed code at runtime. Without this the process dies at
         startup with a Mach exception, exactly as the app does. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The JIT writes to memory pages it then executes; allow-jit alone covers
         MAP_JIT, and CoreCLR maps executable memory beyond that. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- ONNX Runtime's libonnxruntime.dylib arrives signed by its own publisher,
         not by this team. Library validation demands every loaded library match
         the main executable's Team ID, so it has to be off or the engine cannot
         load the very thing it exists to run. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
